{#
  Migration 0103 — CLOSING THE GATE BY EXACTLY ONE.

  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 item 4 · §2.2 findings S8, S19 · §16 MI02, MI29, MI30 ·
  docs/leads/kernel.md rulings D2, D4.

  The mirror of 0101, and the asymmetry between them is the design:

      a new obligation      open_blocking + 1   AND   gate_epoch + 1
      a signed disposition  open_blocking - 1   and NOTHING ELSE

  The epoch increments IFF THE GATE OPENS. Closing an obligation does not move the subject to a
  new (id, epoch) pair, because the pin exists to stop a NEW obligation being attached to a
  COMPLETED transition — and a disposition is not a new obligation. If closing bumped the epoch
  too, a subject that merged at epoch e would be un-mergeable the moment anything was signed
  against it, and the pin would be enforcing the opposite of its purpose.

  RULING D4 — NO `CASE`. §5.11 writes the two counter movements as
  `CASE WHEN NEW.reading_floor_met THEN 0 ELSE 1 END`. `GT-13` has not answered whether a `CASE`
  EXPRESSION is legal inside a PL/pgSQL statement on this platform, and PL-3 forbids resting a
  dated path on an unproven capability. Boolean casts are unconditionally legal, measured working
  on v26.2.5, and read better:

      unmet_floor_count + (NOT (NEW).reading_floor_met)::INT8

  POLARITY IS LOAD-BEARING (finding S19, ruling D1). `reading_floor_met` is POSITIVE — the column
  records that the floor WAS met. `NOT` of it is therefore the thing being counted, and a
  disposition that somehow never reached the projection trigger reads as unmet rather than as
  compliant. Negative polarity would have made an unprojected row look clean.

  THE FLOOR IS PRICED, NOT REFUSED. A breached reading-rate floor never raises. It moves a counter
  the subject's `reading_floor_when_issued` CHECK reads, and that CHECK admits a non-zero unmet
  count when the countersigned count is positive. Fast stays legal; it just names a second,
  differently-credentialed person. Refusing instead would have made the honest measurement of
  deliberation adversarial to the crew, which is how a measurement stops being honest.

  RULING D2 — `override_ledger` IS SUBJECT-POLYMORPHIC. §5.5 gives it `permit_id UUID NOT NULL`,
  which predates finding S16. A change request that trips `weaken_over_blood` and is dispositioned
  by `emergency_override` would be un-insertable, and MI30 — the repository is a protected branch
  — would turn into a brick wall. The insert below therefore names the subject columns of every
  gated subject in this binding.

  WHY THE OVERRIDE ROW IS WRITTEN HERE AND NOT BY THE CALLER. `prior_override_count` is projected
  in 0102 by counting this table, and `override_escalates` compares the projected signer rank
  against `3 + prior_override_count`. If the ledger row were the caller's responsibility, the
  ladder would escalate only against callers who chose to record their own overrides. The count
  and the row must be written by the same mechanism or neither is evidence.

  PLATFORM (measured on CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5, 2026-08-09): `(NEW).col` for every read; boolean
  casts to INT8 inside an UPDATE SET expression are accepted.
#}
{% set ns = namespace(ok=true) %}
{% for s in subjects %}
{%   set inner = namespace(found=false) %}
{%   for c in s.counters %}
{%     if c.column == 'open_blocking' %}{% set inner.found = true %}{% endif %}
{%   endfor %}
{%   if not inner.found %}{% set ns.ok = false %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if not ns.ok %}{{ a_gated_subject_declares_no_open_blocking_counter }}{% endif %}
{% set olcols = [] %}{% for s in subjects %}{% set _ = olcols.append(s.id_column) %}{% endfor %}
{% set olvals = [] %}{% for s in subjects %}{% set _ = olvals.append('(NEW).' ~ s.id_column) %}{% endfor %}
-- @file 0103_fn_disposition_close.sql
{{ header(file='0103_fn_disposition_close.sql',
          title='CREATE FUNCTION ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.fn_disposition_close — one signed disposition closes exactly one obligation',
          rationale='The mirror of the materialisation trigger, and deliberately asymmetric: closing decrements the obligation counter and does NOT bump the epoch, because the epoch pin exists to stop a NEW obligation attaching to a completed transition and a disposition is not one. The reading-rate floor is priced rather than refused — it moves a counter whose CHECK admits a countersignature — and an emergency override writes a ledger row here rather than in the caller, because the count and the row must come from the same mechanism or neither is evidence.',
          mi=['MI02', 'MI29', 'MI30'], i=['I02', 'I10']) }}
--
-- migration:  0103_fn_disposition_close
-- band:       0100-0109z · kernel/projection-triggers · RENDERED
-- statements: 1
-- invariants: MI02 / MI30 (the counter half) · MI29 (overrides escalate against the person
--             across permits, with no ceiling)
-- source:     ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 item 4 · §2.2 S8, S19 · docs/leads/kernel.md D2, D4
-- requires:   {{ binding.schema }}.disposition (0066) · {{ binding.schema }}.override_ledger (0068) ·
{% for s in subjects %}
--             {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} (open_blocking{% for c in s.counters %}{% if c.polarity == 'offset_allowed' %}, {{ c.column }}, countersigned_count{% endif %}{% endfor %})
{% endfor %}
-- provides:   {{ binding.schema }}.fn_disposition_close() — welded to disposition by 0123
-- sqlstate:   P0001 when the disposition names no gated subject. 23514 on the counter
--             non-negativity CHECK if a disposition were ever closed twice.
-- forward-only; no .down.sql and no .up.sql (MR-5).
--
-- COLUMN CONTRACT THIS FILE ASSUMES OF `override_ledger` (ruling D2): site_id, signer_sub,
-- subject_kind, {{ olcols | join(', ') }}, disposition_id — with the two subject-id columns
-- NULLABLE and constrained in pairs, exactly as `blocking_check` and `disposition` are.
--
-- ACYCLICITY. AFTER INSERT on disposition; writes the gated subject tables and override_ledger.
-- override_ledger carries no trigger but the append-only guard (0128 family), which fires on
-- UPDATE and DELETE only. Trigger depth contributed: 1.

CREATE FUNCTION {{ binding.schema }}.fn_disposition_close() RETURNS TRIGGER LANGUAGE PLpgSQL AS $$
BEGIN
{% for s in subjects %}
{%   set floor = namespace(column=none) %}
{%   for c in s.counters %}
{%     if c.polarity == 'offset_allowed' %}{% set floor.column = c.column %}{% endif %}
{%   endfor %}
{%   if loop.first %}
  IF (NEW).{{ s.id_column }} IS NOT NULL THEN
{%   else %}
  ELSIF (NEW).{{ s.id_column }} IS NOT NULL THEN
{%   endif %}
    UPDATE {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }}
       SET open_blocking = open_blocking - 1
{%   if floor.column is not none %}
           -- Finding S19. Positive polarity, so NOT of it is what is counted.
         , {{ floor.column }} = {{ floor.column }} + (NOT (NEW).reading_floor_met)::INT8
         , countersigned_count =
             countersigned_count + ((NEW).countersigner_credential_id IS NOT NULL)::INT8
{%   endif %}
     WHERE {{ s.id_column }} = (NEW).{{ s.id_column }};
{% endfor %}
  ELSE
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE='P0001',
      MESSAGE='{{ binding.name }}: a disposition names no gated subject — nothing to close';
  END IF;

  -- Finding S8. The ladder is site- and signer-scoped and monotone ACROSS subjects; there is no
  -- ceiling and no per-subject reset, because a reset is a bypass with a schedule.
  IF (NEW).kind = 'emergency_override' THEN
    INSERT INTO {{ binding.schema }}.override_ledger
                (site_id, signer_sub, subject_kind, {{ olcols | join(', ') }}, disposition_id)
         VALUES ((NEW).site_id, (NEW).signer_sub, (NEW).subject_kind,
                 {{ olvals | join(', ') }}, (NEW).disposition_id);
  END IF;
  RETURN NEW;
END $$;
